%% Answer plans for every past T2 question — RN volume only.

## Every past question as an answer plan

Use these plans in the last fortnight: read the question, say the plan aloud from memory, then check. Each plan is the skeleton of the full model answer in T2QA.

**2016 Q7 — What is feminism? How is radical feminism different from socialist feminism?** Definition of your own; hooks (2000) and Bhasin and Khan (1986); theory + practice; word from France (Auclert 1882); South Asian roots (Shinde 1882, Rokeya 1905). Common ground (five claims); schools part on cause, site, cure. Radical: *radix*; patriarchy the root; Millett, Firestone, Hanisch (1970); family, body, violence. Socialist: capitalism + patriarchy; Mitchell 1971, Hartmann 1979 (dual systems), Eisenstein 1979. Differences as sub-headings: root cause; unit (sex vs sex and class); view of men; concepts; campaigns; weaknesses; one example read both ways (wife beaten over wages). Compare figure. Shared: deep change, family as site, personal is political, shared history. Pakistan: WAF vs Hudood, laws 2016/2021 (radical); LFPR 24.4%, LHWs, Sindh HBW Act 2018 (socialist). Partners more than rivals.

**2018 Q4 — Which type(s) of feminism can elevate women's socio-cultural, economic and political status in Pakistan?** "Type(s)" = more than one; match schools to the three dimensions. Context: GGGR 2026 143/145, 59.5%; table (11 women on general NA seats 2024, 60 reserved; literacy 68/53; LFPR 24.4%). Liberal → legal/political (Arts 25, 27, 34; laws 2010–2025; quotas; NCSW 2000; limit: enforcement). Islamic → socio-cultural (Wadud, Hassan, Barlas; inheritance, *mahr*, *khula*, consent; MFLO 1961; limit: Zia 2018). Socialist → economic (unpaid farm work, home-based workers, LHWs, BISP 2008; childcare, transport). Radical insight → safety (acid 2011, honour 2016, rape 2021; argue via Art. 14). Men's → gatekeepers (fathers, clerics, police; Rozan; HeForShe; hooks). Intersectionality → reach (rural, poor, minority, disabled, *khawaja siras*). Pyramid figure. Less suitable schools and what each still offers. Plural, home-grown feminism, as in Pakistan's own reforms.

**2019 Q8(b) — Short note: postmodern feminism.** (~560 words.) No single "woman"; identities contingent (could be otherwise) and discursive (made by discourse); meaning of "woman" changes across time and place. Roots: Lyotard 1979 ("incredulity toward metanarratives"), Derrida 1967, Foucault. Thinkers: Butler 1990, Cixous 1975, Flax 1987, Scott 1986/88. Ideas: anti-essentialism and difference; language and literature; suspicion of grand theories; deconstruction of binaries. Compare figure (modern vs postmodern). Strengths; criticisms (Benhabib 1995, Nussbaum 1999, relativism); Spivak's strategic essentialism. Pakistan: many womanhoods; *ghairat* as discourse; Kishwar Naheed. A lens, not a programme.

**2021 Q4 — Development of Marxist/socialist feminism and its stance on gender and exploitation.** Use the question's definition. Five Marxist terms. Stage 1: Engels 1884 (property → monogamy → "world historical defeat"), Bebel 1879, Zetkin 1910, Kollontai. Stage 2: domestic-labour debate — Benston 1969, Dalla Costa and James 1972, Wages for Housework, Federici 1975; social reproduction; cycle figure. Stage 3: Mitchell 1966/71 (four structures), Hartmann 1979 (sex-blind; dual systems; family wage), Eisenstein, Young 1980, Mies 1986, Walby 1990. Stance: materialism; family serves capital; double burden (Hochschild 1989); two exploitations linked; remedy of economic independence and socialised care. Exploitation table (unpaid, cheap, reserve army, global factories). Criticisms + reply (SDG 5.4). Pakistan: LFPR 24.4%, unpaid farm work, HBW Act 2018, LHWs (1994; SC 2012), Sindhiani Tehreek, Okara, BISP.

**2021 Q5 — Gender oppression and women's subordination in psychoanalytic feminism.** Define; from attacking Freud to re-reading him. Psychoanalysis in brief. Freud: Oedipus, castration, penis envy, weaker superego (1925), "anatomy is destiny" (1924). Attack: Horney 1926 (envy of power; womb envy), Beauvoir, Friedan, Millett. Re-reading: Mitchell 1974. Mothering: Dinnerstein 1976 (fear of female power), Chodorow 1978 (relational girls, separating boys; flow figure), Gilligan 1982. Language: Lacan (Law of the Father), Irigaray, Kristeva, Cixous. Analysis table: envy, fear, rejection, self-sacrifice, male-centred culture, internalisation. Correct "inherent": produced and changeable; remedy shared parenting. Criticisms. Pakistan: son preference, "men don't cry", patriarchal bargain (Kandiyoti 1988), involved fathers. Change the nursery as well as the law.

**2022 Q3 — Describe any two Western schools of feminism in detail.** Choose liberal and radical and say why. Each under the same sub-headings. Liberal: meaning (reason, rights, reform); roots (Enlightenment); Wollstonecraft 1792, Seneca Falls 1848, Mill 1869, Friedan 1963, NOW 1966, Okin 1989, Nussbaum 2000; achievements (vote, Equal Pay Act 1963, Title VII 1964, CEDAW 1979/1996); methods; criticisms (structures, private sphere, male norm, class bias, individualism). Radical: *radix*; New Left; NYRW 1967, Redstockings 1969; Millett, Firestone, Hanisch, Brownmiller, Rich; libertarian vs cultural; ideas; achievements (shelters 1971, MacKinnon 1979); criticisms. Compare figure; agree/differ. Pakistan: Arts 25/27/34 and laws (liberal); WAF, laws on violence, Aurat March (radical). The two need each other.

**2024 Q8(b) — Short note: radical feminism.** (~550 words.) *Radix*; patriarchy the deepest oppression; replacing patriarchy = radical. Origin in the late-1960s New Left; NYRW, Redstockings. Millett, Firestone, Hanisch, Brownmiller (dates). Cycle figure of patriarchy. Ideas: sexual politics; personal is political (consciousness-raising); bodily autonomy (Rich 1976); two branches. Achievements (shelters 1971; domestic violence a crime; MacKinnon 1979). Criticisms (race and class; essentialism; anti-men image; backlash; patriarchy not unchanging). Pakistan: WAF vs Hudood 1979; laws 2011/2016/2021; *Mera Jism Meri Marzi*. Analysis powerful; argue it in constitutional and Islamic language.

**2025 Q6 — Intersectionality of gender with other social identities.** Define; Crenshaw 1989; "which women?" Origins: Sojourner Truth 1851; Combahee 1977; *DeGraffenreid v. GM* (1976); traffic image. Ideas: interaction, not addition (bus example); Collins 1990 matrix of domination; privilege and disadvantage together (employer and domestic worker). Venn figure. One heading per identity with Pakistani evidence: class (work, justice); place (literacy ~53%, MMR 186, 2022 floods); religion (reported forced conversions; Ahmadis); ethnicity and caste (Hazara; scheduled castes); disability, age (child marriage, ICT 2025; widows), gender identity (*khawaja siras*, 2018 Act). Summary table. Why it matters (data, policy incl. BISP, movement). Criticisms and Crenshaw's "lens" reply. Begin with the women at the overlaps.
